175
00:20:35,449 --> 00:20:41,919
[Nicholas Gruen] And when we hear of a verdict, we think there, but for the grace of God, go aye.

176
00:20:41,919 --> 00:20:57,769
[Nicholas Gruen] Those are ordinary people, and a very large majority of them, I think, and someone may be able to correct me, but I think in Victoria it's nine out of twelve, have come to a particular conclusion.

177
00:20:57,769 --> 00:20:59,629
[Nicholas Gruen] And that's okay for me.

178
00:20:59,629 --> 00:21:17,582
[Nicholas Gruen] And so I think there is something somehow mistaken in the idea that a democracy involves that there's a sort of an image, it's not explicitly said, but there's a sort of an image in which everyone's involved in everything.

179
00:21:17,582 --> 00:21:20,413
[Nicholas Gruen] And it's obvious that that's wrong.

180
00:21:20,413 --> 00:21:23,728
[Nicholas Gruen] Uh there needs to be a division of labor.

181
00:21:23,728 --> 00:21:37,800
[Nicholas Gruen] And so we've been sold this kind of a populist idea of democracy that we should all be involved in everything, and there's just a kind of cacophony all directed at the media.

182
00:21:37,800 --> 00:21:47,019
[Nicholas Gruen] And what I want, you know, I don't ever want to go to a meeting about how garbage is collected in my neighborhood.

183
00:21:47,019 --> 00:21:56,966
[Nicholas Gruen] I will go if it's part of my democratic duty, but I don't want any more people than necessary to go to sort that thing out.

184
00:21:56,966 --> 00:22:01,090
[Nicholas Gruen] And there are a million things that governments do.

185
00:22:01,090 --> 00:22:12,088
[Nicholas Gruen] So what I want is I want some confidence that something's happened, now we're only collecting garbage once every two weeks or something.

186
00:22:12,088 --> 00:22:22,359
[Nicholas Gruen] And I want there to be some, I want to have some faith that that decision, whether I agree with it or not, was reasonably made.

187
00:22:22,359 --> 00:22:53,251
[Nicholas Gruen] And mostly we don't have that sense because we have a politician yapping at us with some uh talking points, and then we have some inane journalists trying to trip them up, and then we have the opposition saying it's all terrible, but if they were in government, they'd be saying what the government's saying, and the current government would be saying what the opposition is saying.

188
00:22:53,251 --> 00:23:00,205
[Nicholas Gruen] So it's a complete panto, and it's and people can see that that is not worthy of them.

189
00:23:00,205 --> 00:23:04,087
[Nicholas Gruen] Um and so, yeah, that's my contribution to that.

190
00:23:04,087 --> 00:23:12,174
[Nicholas Gruen] That yes, a democracy does need an involved citizenry, it needs a virtuous citizenry to some extent.

191
00:23:12,174 --> 00:23:24,547
[Nicholas Gruen] But it doesn't, it absolutely doesn't mean that we all have to be busily um, you know, watching all the current affairs shows and all that sort of stuff.

192
00:23:24,547 --> 00:23:31,583
[Nicholas Gruen] Um I'll just leave you with a thought um by a guy uh called Pope and his son Hugh Pope.

193
00:23:31,583 --> 00:23:43,632
[Nicholas Gruen] I'm telling you like this because I've forgotten the Christian name of the man who wrote the book, but his son Hugh published this book, he found it.

194
00:23:43,632 --> 00:23:45,897
[Nicholas Gruen] Um and and he quotes um G.K.

195
00:23:45,897 --> 00:23:54,469
[Nicholas Gruen] Chesterton saying that democracy is about giving the shy people as much of a say as the blowhards like me.

196
00:23:54,469 --> 00:23:55,520
[Nicholas Gruen] I'll pass on.

197
00:23:57,360 --> 00:24:01,051
[Nicole] I think your question was, will tech save us?

198
00:24:01,051 --> 00:24:03,102
[Nicole] Um I think my view is no.

199
00:24:03,102 --> 00:24:07,778
[Nicole] But it's it's also gonna it's also gonna help and hinder.

200
00:24:07,778 --> 00:24:09,583
[Nicole] That would be my view.

201
00:24:09,583 --> 00:24:25,579
[Nicole] I think that we have we are in an environment that means we are going, we can have conversations across the seas online with each other in real time and not have to fly everyone around the world.

202
00:24:25,579 --> 00:24:29,845
[Nicole] Um, we can and we can that helps, that's technology.

203
00:24:29,845 --> 00:24:32,060
[Nicole] But it's not gonna save us.

204
00:24:32,060 --> 00:24:53,552
[Nicole] Like um, no matter whether we use AI or we're using an app or we're doing a survey or we're using some polis or whatever it is we're doing, all of those, all of those technologies, any technology, be it a method in a room, will exclude people and include people.

205
00:24:53,552 --> 00:24:58,884
[Nicole] So, you know, it's a it's a suite of things we've got to Include.

206
00:24:58,884 --> 00:25:05,037
[Nicole] And I think we've got to think carefully about the barriers at every point.

207
00:25:05,037 --> 00:25:17,342
[Nicole] I totally agree with Nicholas that not everybody has to be involved in every conversation and we need to think carefully about methodology to do that.

208
00:25:17,342 --> 00:25:22,510
[Nicole] I also think we are at a serious turning point with technology.

209
00:25:22,510 --> 00:25:25,545
[Nicole] The news today is an example of that.

210
00:25:25,545 --> 00:25:36,783
[Nicole] There are many people, and I have watched a so-called deliberation at a global level that's been run by Meta that's completely automated.

211
00:25:36,783 --> 00:25:54,174
[Nicole] There are people from around the world, it is an automated task, you get a time, you fill out a thing, you move on, you go on to the next task, and you watch it in the background, and it is terrifying in my eyes.

212
00:25:54,174 --> 00:25:57,538
[Nicole] Because there is no meeting of the minds.

213
00:25:57,538 --> 00:26:02,131
[Nicole] They are ticking off boxes and moving through a process.

214
00:26:02,131 --> 00:26:07,463
[Nicole] And the context we're in now is we have to understand difference.

215
00:26:07,463 --> 00:26:17,061
[Nicole] Take time to sit and talk to people who think differently to us, otherwise, we will tribalize more and more and more.

216
00:26:17,061 --> 00:26:21,573
[Nicole] And social media is just amplifying that at the moment.

217
00:26:21,573 --> 00:26:29,120
[Nicole] So we just need to be really careful with any technology we're using to support the process.

218
00:26:32,640 --> 00:26:36,183
[Godfrey] I think Samuel Johnson wrote, Reason dies alone.

219
00:26:36,183 --> 00:26:50,135
[Godfrey] And the issue with technology is that it displaces social cooperation, which is kind of bizarre in the sense that the technology only exists because of that pre-existing social cooperation.

220
00:26:50,135 --> 00:26:56,262
[Godfrey] And we can see that when people try and think of solutions in the public discourse.

221
00:26:56,262 --> 00:26:58,698
[Godfrey] You mentioned it before, Anthony.

222
00:26:58,698 --> 00:27:00,470
[Godfrey] There's an app for that.

223
00:27:00,470 --> 00:27:06,892
[Godfrey] So often now we think what's the technical solution, what's the technological solution?

224
00:27:06,892 --> 00:27:17,079
[Godfrey] And there's not a discussion or a discourse about what is the structural or organizational solution that we need to bring to bear on that.

225
00:27:17,079 --> 00:27:29,924
[Godfrey] So we've we've we got organized enough to create this technology and now it's preventing us in a way from reorganizing in response to changed facts and changed circumstances.

226
00:27:29,924 --> 00:27:39,963
[Godfrey] And I think that is that is the issue that technology has a place, but we've got to get the prior question of organization done firstly.

227
00:27:39,963 --> 00:27:43,580
[Godfrey] And the other factor in relation to that is time.

228
00:27:43,580 --> 00:27:58,418
[Godfrey] We just capitalism has found a way to take and rob us of time, whether it's through the smartphones that we're addicted to in our pocket or or trying to find the next gig or job or whatever else it is.

229
00:27:58,418 --> 00:28:13,920
[Godfrey] And we need a way of fighting for shorter working hours, but in a in a way that means that people can live a life that is economically secure and have the time so that participation is not bounded by privilege.

230
00:28:19,680 --> 00:28:24,111
[Anitra] I'm not sure whether I've got anything extra to add to that.

231
00:28:24,111 --> 00:28:24,480
[Anitra] Yeah.

232
00:28:25,440 --> 00:28:27,951
[Anthony (Host)] Nothing wrong with a bit of economy.

233
00:28:27,951 --> 00:28:28,928
[Anthony (Host)] No, thank you.

234
00:28:28,928 --> 00:28:33,253
[Anthony (Host)] And yeah, so I've I do have a question though for you, Anitra.

235
00:28:33,253 --> 00:28:39,182
[Anthony (Host)] Um so you work on this thing called degrowth, which may not be familiar to everybody.

236
00:28:39,182 --> 00:28:44,483
[Anthony (Host)] Um, and you talked a little bit about being a part of collaborative housing.

237
00:28:44,483 --> 00:28:47,553
[Anthony (Host)] Um and yeah, housing's a really tricky area.

238
00:28:47,553 --> 00:28:59,690
[Anthony (Host)] I've done work in that neck of the woods, and and everyone, well not everyone, but some might have an experience of a thing called an owner's corporation, um, body corporate.

239
00:28:59,690 --> 00:29:20,338
[Anthony (Host)] So yeah, it's a it's a tricky area when people are making decisions around their living arrangements and how they live together, whether it's in close quarters or even just talking about common infrastructure like a block of flats, and then if you're renting, you just cut out of that altogether.

240
00:29:20,338 --> 00:29:27,314
[Anthony (Host)] But um yeah, there's you've also talked a lot about being less dependent on this thing called money.

241
00:29:27,314 --> 00:29:35,615
[Anthony (Host)] Um, and you know, back in the 90s it was, you know, use your money as your vote, these sorts of things were being said.

242
00:29:35,615 --> 00:29:55,984
[Anthony (Host)] So um I'm wondering whether from your experience and um your interest in this sort of more horizontal decision making, um if you can see some indicators for where we can best go in such a divided and polarized world, which we've we've started to talk about, and the relationship between that.

243
00:29:55,984 --> 00:30:07,215
[Anthony (Host)] And the way we use money, you know, like um often those two things we talk about making decisions together as if this thing called money doesn't exist out of it.

244
00:30:07,215 --> 00:30:17,120
[Anthony (Host)] So I'm really interested in your thoughts because you've looked at those deeply, how those two things are connected and where we can maybe go.

245
00:30:18,880 --> 00:30:19,993
[Anitra] Yeah, okay.

246
00:30:19,993 --> 00:30:23,535
[Anitra] I became really interested in this.

247
00:30:23,535 --> 00:30:51,769
[Anitra] Um, in my undergraduate years, I did Latin American studies and uh I was doing a history major and decided that in fact Latin American history and the Australian history that I was aware of really boiled down to economic history, and so that's what really drew me into economics.

248
00:30:51,769 --> 00:30:58,044
[Anitra] And um I ended up doing a PhD on Karl Marx's concept of money.

249
00:30:58,044 --> 00:31:32,147
[Anitra] Um so uh by that time, um, and I'd had a long involvement in both environmental movement as well as women's movement, and women's movement has a lot of horizontalism, which I can come back to, but that was my introduction to horizontalism, having assemblies and working groups and people really self-organizing, a lot of self-governance.

250
00:31:32,147 --> 00:31:59,977
[Anitra] The kind of position that I'd come to was that we have all these big social problems of inequities between people, and we have growing in ecological crises, and every time that we look at these, examine these kind of problems, people seem to come back to economic conundrums.

251
00:31:59,977 --> 00:32:09,995
[Anitra] Um if you take monetary relationships out of the picture, um it can be it can solve a few problems.

252
00:32:09,995 --> 00:32:16,067
[Anitra] So, number one, inequities can regenerate it within society.

253
00:32:16,067 --> 00:32:36,914
[Anitra] One of the reasons that I think that it's been really difficult for the women's movement to move has been that okay, you can give people opportunities when they're in their 30s or their forties or whatever.

254
00:32:36,914 --> 00:33:03,023
[Anitra] Um, but the elite, the the patriarchy, as it were, still has is so far ahead that, and because the way that money works and investments and accumulation, um any group that's actually given an opportunity is only kind of like going to make very slow progress.

255
00:33:03,023 --> 00:33:15,066
[Anitra] So I recognize that monetary economies very much generate and make more complex all of the inequities within societies.

256
00:33:15,066 --> 00:33:18,000
[Anitra] Money is about more and less.

257
00:33:18,000 --> 00:33:21,644
[Anitra] It's about competition between that.

258
00:33:21,644 --> 00:33:24,781
[Anitra] That's the whole dynamic of it.

259
00:33:24,781 --> 00:33:47,651
[Anitra] Um, in terms of ecology, um the biggest problems that we kind of face now today is sort of like Australia is in a position where the average Australian is producing andor consuming at the rate of there being about five Earths.

260
00:33:47,651 --> 00:33:58,986
[Anitra] We really have to be all collectively making decisions about our futures, which means that we get back on track.

261
00:33:58,986 --> 00:34:02,224
[Anitra] We have to do that collectively.

262
00:34:02,224 --> 00:34:06,171
[Anitra] We actually have to be more democratic.

263
00:34:06,171 --> 00:34:15,582
[Anitra] There's no bigger kind of um process or organizational challenge than that as I see it today.

264
00:34:15,582 --> 00:34:21,654
[Anitra] So I can talk about horizontalism and stuff later, but yeah.

265
00:34:21,654 --> 00:34:22,160
[Anitra] Yeah.

266
00:34:22,720 --> 00:34:39,695
[Anthony (Host)] So just as a little follow-up, because uh in the co-opi neck of the woods, um there's uh uh essay from the 1970s women's movement that I came across through the work of Nathan Schneider, who's um quite active in the cooperative movement globally.

267
00:34:39,695 --> 00:34:56,394
[Anthony (Host)] And it was interesting this this essay called The Tyranny of Structurelessness by Jo Freeman talks about her experience in the 1970s of being in these um feminine collectives that looked very, very flat, and she's really excited and got into.

268
00:34:56,394 --> 00:34:57,567
[Anthony (Host)] Involved in them.

269
00:34:57,567 --> 00:35:10,195
[Anthony (Host)] And then after a little while I sort of realized actually there's people making decisions here, but I don't actually know how they're making decisions and who the decision makers are.

270
00:35:10,195 --> 00:35:14,128
[Anthony (Host)] So she started to talk about the importance of structure.

271
00:35:14,128 --> 00:35:22,753
[Anthony (Host)] So when you talk about something like horizontalism, it might sound like choose your own adventure and have a bit of a scrum.

272
00:35:22,753 --> 00:35:30,689
[Anthony (Host)] Whereas what Jo Freeman was saying, that you need to be really clear about who's doing what and who's accountable.

273
00:35:30,689 --> 00:35:37,520
[Anthony (Host)] So I'm just wondering if, on a sort of a practical level, whether you'd be able to comment on that.

274
00:35:38,240 --> 00:35:48,197
[Anitra] Well, it's very similar because a lot of people misrepresent anarchy as being chaos or just complete freedom.

275
00:35:48,197 --> 00:35:51,029
[Anitra] Anyone does whatever they like.

276
00:35:51,029 --> 00:35:53,769
[Anitra] And it's not like that at all.

277
00:35:53,769 --> 00:35:56,875
[Anitra] Anarchy is really self-governance.

278
00:35:56,875 --> 00:36:06,010
[Anitra] And you have to have processes and they have to be transparent and everything has to be accountable.

279
00:36:06,010 --> 00:36:40,814
[Anitra] So when I lived in a commune, we had a meeting every Monday morning, and we divided up all the tasks that needed to be done through the week for both the kind of enterprise that the commune partly lived off, and all of the more self collectively self-sufficient kind of growing vegetables, looking after children, all of those kinds of housework jobs and all of that kind of thing.

280
00:36:40,814 --> 00:36:43,371
[Anitra] And that's direct democracy.

281
00:36:43,371 --> 00:36:56,708
[Anitra] That's you know, and we I mean, it wasn't as if people just decided that they'd do any old job and that they'd work when they felt like it at all.

282
00:36:56,708 --> 00:37:05,752
[Anitra] It was like we had set rules over what kind of work was work, what kind of activities weren't work.

283
00:37:05,752 --> 00:37:16,348
[Anitra] Um so uh I think that probably in the last 50 years there's been a lot more clarity over um structure in that sense.

284
00:37:16,348 --> 00:37:28,315
[Anitra] And for me, I can't see much structure in the worlds of Boris Johnson, Donald Trump, and all of these kind of mainstream democracy.

285
00:37:28,315 --> 00:37:42,474
[Anitra] I can't see a structure like that's really scary, and you know, it seems to be a little bit like the kind of structuralistness argument that people get at.

286
00:37:42,474 --> 00:38:06,864
[Anitra] And what that structuralist argument is really getting at is that under certain circumstances, when people aren't treated as if they're equal and there's just an equal space for them to speak and act and have power over people, well then there's a really big problem.

287
00:38:06,864 --> 00:38:14,720
[Anitra] And that's what we've got a lot of the time within capitalism and capitalist politics.

288
00:38:18,080 --> 00:38:28,509
[Anthony (Host)] Yes, flooding the zone, so we're just um thinking that everyone's having a say, but really um it's just a lot of crap, really, um, where we're at.

289
00:38:28,509 --> 00:38:34,223
[Anthony (Host)] But we're trying to get out of the crap a bit, and I'm looking across at Nicole.

290
00:38:34,223 --> 00:38:50,080
[Anthony (Host)] Um, so we've gone from Commune, so I'm gonna go to the Yarra Valley Water Citizens Jury because that's a really practical um grassroots process that you were involved with, um, and heard lots of fantastic things about it.

291
00:38:50,080 --> 00:38:57,651
[Anthony (Host)] Now, this is where so-called ordinary people come together and talk about water and try to make decisions.

292
00:38:57,651 --> 00:39:02,080
[Anthony (Host)] Could I really fascinated in terms of how that actually works.

293
00:39:05,360 --> 00:39:09,351
[Nicole] I could talk for three weeks on this, so I'll be really quick.

294
00:39:09,351 --> 00:39:21,324
[Nicole] So, um, first and foremost, it is a random selection of people from the community, or in this case the customer base for Yarra Valley Water, stratified against what that community is like.

295
00:39:21,324 --> 00:39:24,671
[Nicole] So it's a mini version of the customers in the room.

296
00:39:24,671 --> 00:39:30,657
[Nicole] So age, gender, location, uh could be background, depending on whatever the filters might be.

297
00:39:30,657 --> 00:39:38,510
[Nicole] So we randomly select two-stage random selection to get a group of people in the room that represents their customer base.

298
00:39:38,510 --> 00:39:43,145
[Nicole] And then we spend a lot of time with them, um, at least 30% of the time.

299
00:39:43,145 --> 00:39:51,771
[Nicole] And in the case of Yarra Valley Water, uh, there's a couple of times we've been through this, we spend at least six days all together.

300
00:39:51,771 --> 00:39:53,702
[Nicole] Half of that time is learning.

301
00:39:53,702 --> 00:40:02,392
[Nicole] Learn, learn, learn, learn, learn, understand, dialogue, ask questions, pick your own speakers, um, uh really to interrogate what this.

302
00:40:02,392 --> 00:40:05,481
[Nicole] Thing is, which is how much does our water cost?

303
00:40:05,481 --> 00:40:14,493
[Nicole] And then you take time throwing up ideas on we agree with this, this should be the way it should be priced, we think this is most important.

304
00:40:14,493 --> 00:40:19,063
[Nicole] You make decisions, they write their own the report, they hand it over.

305
00:40:19,063 --> 00:40:26,788
[Nicole] The organization then says we will include this to the maximum extent possible in our price submission to the regulator.

306
00:40:26,788 --> 00:40:28,526
[Nicole] So that's it in a nutshell.

307
00:40:28,526 --> 00:40:32,517
[Nicole] There's obviously lots of parts to that, lots of ins and outs.

308
00:40:32,517 --> 00:40:39,790
[Nicole] And one of the biggest bits, the most enjoyable bits, is seeing the transformation from the beginning to the end.

309
00:40:39,790 --> 00:40:46,678
[Nicole] Most people who come in in these processes are unknown to the organization because of the random selection.

310
00:40:46,678 --> 00:40:58,264
[Nicole] And for me, that is the biggest change in the way we engage with our communities because it's nicer to get an invitation than to just, you know, turn up to the things you normally.

311
00:40:58,264 --> 00:41:02,899
[Nicole] You know, if you get an invitation to a party, you're more likely to go.

312
00:41:02,899 --> 00:41:07,920
[Nicole] But if there was just a notification of a party, you're not going to go to it.

313
00:41:07,920 --> 00:41:09,658
[Nicole] So that makes a difference.

314
00:41:09,658 --> 00:41:10,301
[Nicole] Pardon me.

315
00:41:10,301 --> 00:41:14,228
[Nicole] Um, the second bit is we spend a lot of time on brain biases.

316
00:41:14,228 --> 00:41:16,674
[Nicole] How do we naturally bias our thinking?

317
00:41:16,674 --> 00:41:20,150
[Nicole] Therefore, how do we do critical thinking differently?

318
00:41:20,150 --> 00:41:26,008
[Nicole] And how do we elevate that in the way we live and work, and particularly in this situation?

319
00:41:26,008 --> 00:41:30,642
[Nicole] And last but not least, they make decisions, they that write the report.

320
00:41:30,642 --> 00:41:33,732
[Nicole] There's not a report given to them that's blank.

321
00:41:33,732 --> 00:41:46,027
[Nicole] And to hear the sorts of things you hear at the end of a process like this, I have run hundreds of these things, but at the end of it, you hear people say, you know, this has changed my life.

322
00:41:46,027 --> 00:41:49,503
[Nicole] I've had people sign up to these things for the money.

323
00:41:49,503 --> 00:41:55,618
[Nicole] I had one woman, I'll tell her story because it's beautiful, it was for an energy organization.

324
00:41:55,618 --> 00:41:59,609
[Nicole] This woman signed up because she needed to get her teeth done.

325
00:41:59,609 --> 00:42:08,234
[Nicole] So she signed up to get the money because you pay people a stipend, it's minimum wage to be in the room, which I think is fair enough.

326
00:42:08,234 --> 00:42:10,294
[Nicole] They give a fair amount of time.

327
00:42:10,294 --> 00:42:13,770
[Nicole] Oh my god, she was the best participant I've ever had.

328
00:42:13,770 --> 00:42:16,345
[Nicole] She struggled with every single concept.

329
00:42:16,345 --> 00:42:23,297
[Nicole] She did the homework, she turned up, she challenged everyone in the room, and they got a really good result.

330
00:42:23,297 --> 00:42:28,768
[Nicole] And she is an advocate for that organization to this day because it changed her life.

331
00:42:28,768 --> 00:42:34,883
[Nicole] And I kid you not, just about at every closing session I run for a deliberative process, I cry.

332
00:42:34,883 --> 00:42:38,488
[Nicole] I'm that emotional, because you see the shift in people.

333
00:42:38,488 --> 00:42:40,998
[Nicole] They are like, I can make a difference.

334
00:42:40,998 --> 00:42:41,900
[Nicole] I am powerful.

335
00:42:41,900 --> 00:42:43,444
[Nicole] I've worked with others.

336
00:42:43,444 --> 00:42:44,861
[Nicole] This is a great thing.

337
00:42:44,861 --> 00:42:46,470
[Nicole] We could do more of this.

338
00:42:46,470 --> 00:42:47,822
[Nicole] This is how it works.

339
00:42:47,822 --> 00:42:51,040
[Nicole] And they say, and they say, and this is democracy.

340
00:42:51,520 --> 00:42:58,240
[Nicholas Gruen] Not not the not the 730 report with Sarah Ferguson trying to trap whoever she's trying to trap that night.

341
00:42:59,360 --> 00:43:01,899
[Nicole] And it's the it's they're really diverse, right?

342
00:43:01,899 --> 00:43:05,971
[Nicole] These people are really different and have very different views on the world.

343
00:43:05,971 --> 00:43:15,120
[Nicole] And they come to a meeting of the minds because you take the time, you slow it down, you learn about stuff, you talk to each other, you meet people who are different to you.

344
00:43:19,680 --> 00:43:33,086
[Anthony (Host)] Well, speaking of energy, Godfrey, uh you are a co-founder of Co-Power, uh, and members of that cooperative uh and people associated with it make decisions about what happens with some of the surplus.

345
00:43:33,086 --> 00:43:47,230
[Anthony (Host)] So it's an interesting democratic um enterprise that it's dealing with money that Anitra's been talking about, and it's dealing with actually doing, you know, making decisions together about what's done with it.

346
00:43:47,230 --> 00:43:51,654
[Anthony (Host)] You'll you've also recently finished with um United Workers Union.

347
00:43:51,654 --> 00:44:01,105
[Anthony (Host)] Uh, you've written in your Substack about members in unions being part of the decision-making process and some of the challenges around that.

348
00:44:01,105 --> 00:44:09,484
[Anthony (Host)] So you've been involved in doing something about decision making, and and um you've also been involved in the union movement.

349
00:44:09,484 --> 00:44:23,092
[Anthony (Host)] So I'm interested in the dialogue between those two things and whether you want to have a more of a discussion about how maybe unions can be a little bit more democratic and how co-ops could fit into it.

350
00:44:23,092 --> 00:44:25,840
[Anthony (Host)] Wherever you want to go with it, Godfrey.

351
00:44:30,720 --> 00:44:31,250
[Godfrey] Thanks.

352
00:44:31,250 --> 00:44:45,853
[Godfrey] Um so what I would say about Co-Power is we uh Tom and a few other comrades, we we set it up as an intervention in the market to try and eat the market and and return value back to the commons.

353
00:44:45,853 --> 00:44:51,377
[Godfrey] It's a big ambition, we've got a very long way to go in relation to that.

354
00:44:51,377 --> 00:45:17,481
[Godfrey] But um we I think this this issue about money and elections is really important and the work that Nicole's talked about in uh citizens' assemblies and water authorities, because we thought there was an opportunity with Copower because of how privatized and corporatized, at least in Victoria, the retail end of the electricity market had become.

355
00:45:17,481 --> 00:45:25,048
[Godfrey] You see your electricity company on the television, you are literally paying for that on your bills.

356
00:45:25,048 --> 00:45:33,068
[Godfrey] It might not be expressly itemized in your bills, but it's an allowable charge that retailers can pass on.

357
00:45:33,068 --> 00:45:46,461
[Godfrey] So when we're talking about the resources that it would take in order to transition our energy systems over to something that is at least a lot more sustainable into the future.

358
00:45:46,461 --> 00:46:01,594
[Godfrey] When you see a billboard, when you see a TV ad or something else, you are seeing resources being spread around in a vastly inefficient manner, money being spread around in a vastly inefficient manner.

359
00:46:01,594 --> 00:46:04,318
[Godfrey] And that's the system that we're in.

360
00:46:04,318 --> 00:46:10,371
[Godfrey] If you read Aristotle's politics, elections weren't in the section on democracy.

361
00:46:10,371 --> 00:46:13,625
[Godfrey] Elections were in the section on oligarchy.

362
00:46:13,625 --> 00:46:22,856
[Godfrey] Because it was an institution that was, uh, in Aristotle's view, uh best suited to those who had the most amount of money.

363
00:46:22,856 --> 00:46:31,936
[Godfrey] And the only way through that is how do we build systems of direct democracy, which requires structure and organization.

364
00:46:31,936 --> 00:46:55,089
[Godfrey] And I've spent most of my working life thinking through how do we get people organized and spend far too little of my working life thinking through what does that organization look like at the end, which I think is a vital question for a vital question for our age, because the labor movement is in crisis.

365
00:46:55,089 --> 00:47:23,312
[Godfrey] We do have an issue there about uh workers being alienated from the labor movement, being distant because of its entanglement in parliamentary politics and inherently oligarchic systems of parliamentary elections, which is part of the pre-existing um lack of confidence, I think, within the movement to build power in the workplace, build power within everyday communities.

366
00:47:23,312 --> 00:47:28,685
[Godfrey] Uh and I won't go into you know, I've I've written quite a bit on that.

367
00:47:28,685 --> 00:47:33,830
[Godfrey] We could have a whole uh TED talk on it, but what I would say, yeah.

368
00:47:33,830 --> 00:47:35,192
[Godfrey] Yeah, fair enough.

369
00:47:35,192 --> 00:47:41,926
[Godfrey] Um what I would say is this one thing that we need to divest ourselves of in authorities.

370
00:47:41,926 --> 00:48:04,323
[Godfrey] I think most of us walk around with a mental model in our in our head around authority of top-down structures, and those top-down structures uh exist in a vacuum or maybe on like a billiard board table when really we're all nested within subsystems and systems and broader systems and ecosystems.

371
00:48:04,323 --> 00:48:19,759
[Godfrey] And we need to get that Western capitalist mindset out of our out of our head, which is probably an overhang from the Catholic Church and the Roman Empire, with you know King Charles up on the wall there.

372
00:48:19,759 --> 00:48:23,466
[Godfrey] Hierarchy structure isn't dependent on hierarchy.

373
00:48:23,466 --> 00:48:26,947
[Godfrey] Authority shouldn't be dependent on hierarchy.

374
00:48:26,947 --> 00:48:57,743
[Godfrey] I think the best leadership and best authority exists when it exists on the border between systems, where you have people, structures, and collectives that act as translators between needs of various systems, whether it's the ecosystem and the village, whether it's um whether it's an enterprise and and an industry, whether it's a local community and a broader uh ecosystem or like a river delta community.

375
00:48:57,743 --> 00:49:12,800
[Godfrey] We need to think through authority as about people existing on borders and bringing that knowledge and communication channel for more deliberative structures and um and direct participation in place.

376
00:49:18,800 --> 00:49:22,789
[Anthony (Host)] So Nicholas, I had uh some questions about citizens' juries.

377
00:49:22,789 --> 00:49:56,432
[Anthony (Host)] We've already sort of gone there a little bit, um, and I think I think Godfrey raised some really interesting things, and I thought, oh gee, maybe maybe Nicholas might want to respond to some of the things that Godfrey's just said there around the nature of democracy, or you might want to dig deeper into um the idea of these um citizens' juries and how kind of you've already outlined how kind of toxic representative democracies become, and I think you know you just have to watch TV to agree with that.

378
00:49:56,432 --> 00:50:06,007
[Anthony (Host)] So yeah, I'm interested in Yeah, where you want to go, you might want to respond to some of the things that other panelists have have mentioned.

379
00:50:06,007 --> 00:50:13,520
[Anthony (Host)] Um, and you might want to dig deep into the citizens' juries, or yeah, just generally the economics of the thing.

380
00:50:14,560 --> 00:50:18,291
[Nicholas Gruen] Uh no, I won't talk about the economics of it.

381
00:50:18,291 --> 00:50:23,644
[Nicholas Gruen] Um, but I will take you up on the on um following on from Godfrey.

382
00:50:23,644 --> 00:50:29,889
[Nicholas Gruen] I was very pleased that Godfrey mentioned my close personal friend Aristotle.

383
00:50:29,889 --> 00:50:37,107
[Nicholas Gruen] Um, and Aristotle, as he rightly said, described elections as aristocratic or oligarchic.

384
00:50:37,107 --> 00:50:44,650
[Nicholas Gruen] Oligarchic now, Aristotle's three forms of government uh exist in a good form and a bad form.

385
00:50:44,650 --> 00:50:53,247
[Nicholas Gruen] And the bad form is always when the people in power make it about themselves rather than about the public.

386
00:50:53,247 --> 00:51:11,982
[Nicholas Gruen] And that can happen to a kingship, which becomes a tyranny, it can happen to an aristocracy, which then becomes an oligarchy, and it can happen to a democracy, which then becomes what he called an ochlocracy, the rule of the worst.

387
00:51:11,982 --> 00:51:21,552
[Nicholas Gruen] Now, the other thing that Aristotle said, he didn't just say that elections were naturally aristocratic or oligarchic.

388
00:51:21,552 --> 00:51:24,310
[Nicholas Gruen] Let's understand why he said that.

389
00:51:24,310 --> 00:51:26,987
[Nicholas Gruen] Oligarchy is the rule by the few.

390
00:51:26,987 --> 00:51:31,934
[Nicholas Gruen] And if you hold an election, that's what you're trying to do.

391
00:51:31,934 --> 00:51:38,584
[Nicholas Gruen] You're trying to get the consent of the many to choose a few who will govern them.

392
00:51:38,584 --> 00:51:57,806
[Nicholas Gruen] There are all kinds of ways in which that's absolutely necessary for social functioning, and there was an Italian-German theorist of the turn of the 20th century called Robert Michels, who coined the expression the iron law of oligarchy.

393
00:51:57,806 --> 00:52:07,944
[Nicholas Gruen] And he showed how as organizations become more complex, you can't spend all your time going back to plenaries the whole time.

394
00:52:07,944 --> 00:52:10,215
[Nicholas Gruen] You have to have structures.

395
00:52:10,215 --> 00:52:19,948
[Nicholas Gruen] And I think Anitra was saying something a little bit similar, and that article about uh that you referred to is similar.

396
00:52:19,948 --> 00:52:21,976
[Nicholas Gruen] So, what do elections do?

397
00:52:21,976 --> 00:52:25,706
[Nicholas Gruen] They separate the governed from the governing.

398
00:52:25,706 --> 00:52:28,545
[Nicholas Gruen] They are competitive by definition.

399
00:52:28,545 --> 00:52:40,548
[Nicholas Gruen] And Aristotle said something that I thought think is really sublime about this alternative in uh institution, which is selection by lot, by lottery.

400
00:52:40,548 --> 00:52:56,202
[Nicholas Gruen] Um he said that that is an that is uh a quintessentially democratic uh institution, and it involves people taking turns in governing and being governed, which is exactly what happens in juries.

401
00:52:56,202 --> 00:53:04,555
[Nicholas Gruen] And so it doesn't separate the governed from the governing except in so far as is absolutely necessary.

402
00:53:04,555 --> 00:53:15,667
[Nicholas Gruen] It doesn't disturb the essential equality of people while accepting that there is a division of cognitive labor in any complex situation.

403
00:53:15,667 --> 00:53:29,049
[Nicholas Gruen] Somebody has to be on that jury, twelve people have to be on that jury, and they have to sit through twelve days or whatever it is, and then they deliberate on that.

404
00:53:29,049 --> 00:53:43,567
[Nicholas Gruen] And it's completely absurd to have a hundred people doing it or everyone doing it, although that's kind of what we do at the same time for our own entertainment through the press.

405
00:53:43,567 --> 00:53:59,544
[Nicholas Gruen] So these very simple ways of thinking about the structures that govern us have really influenced me very strongly because I do think that, I mean, Anthony let the expression drop just a minute ago.

406
00:53:59,544 --> 00:54:05,060
[Nicholas Gruen] He talked about representative democracy as if that meant elections.

407
00:54:05,060 --> 00:54:22,092
[Nicholas Gruen] No, it can, you can talk about representative democracy and everyone does, as if that presupposes elections, but it could presuppose the alternative way to represent the people, which is what happens in juries.

408
00:54:22,092 --> 00:54:35,961
[Nicholas Gruen] Representation by sampling rather than representation by elections, with all of the profound differences that that involves to equality, to the framing of what's going on.

409
00:54:35,961 --> 00:54:41,557
[Nicholas Gruen] If you set up a competition, you'll get a certain kind of discussion.

410
00:54:41,557 --> 00:54:59,968
[Nicholas Gruen] And we can see it every day because not only is our political system um profoundly competitive, but the medium through which it travels to us, the media, has always been competitive and is now much more competitive than it was.

411
00:54:59,968 --> 00:55:12,133
[Nicholas Gruen] And it's more or Less doing to our political system what happens to a fly when you spray morteen on it, which is that you dissolve its nervous system.

412
00:55:12,133 --> 00:55:12,944
[Nicholas Gruen] Just dead.

413
00:55:12,944 --> 00:55:15,377
[Nicholas Gruen] So that's a bunch of thoughts.

414
00:55:15,377 --> 00:55:24,786
[Nicholas Gruen] I'll just say one other thing, which is that Nicole spoke, well, about the tears in her eyes, I know what she means.

415
00:55:24,786 --> 00:55:38,573
[Nicholas Gruen] She spoke about the fact that you can't run one of these exercises well, which Nicole does, without producing the kind of transformation in the people that she mentioned.

416
00:55:38,573 --> 00:55:44,818
[Nicholas Gruen] And the way I put that is that we evolved on the African savannah to do that.

417
00:55:44,818 --> 00:55:49,604
[Nicholas Gruen] And when people discover it, it completely astonishes them.

418
00:55:49,604 --> 00:55:57,876
[Nicholas Gruen] And I wouldn't be going on about democracy so much if I just thought this was a terribly hard problem.

419
00:55:57,876 --> 00:55:59,417
[Nicholas Gruen] It's not that hard.

420
00:55:59,417 --> 00:56:12,313
[Nicholas Gruen] Because this other way to represent the people just sits there, and every opportunity it's given performs in an astonishing way, if you get it all close to it.

421
00:56:12,313 --> 00:56:28,858
[Nicholas Gruen] But we don't, so it would be with the there is more than enough philanthropic money around for us to set up a standing citizen assembly of a hundred odd people that simply shadowed the federal parliament.

422
00:56:28,858 --> 00:56:31,535
[Nicholas Gruen] No, don't ask for any permission.

423
00:56:31,535 --> 00:56:38,266
[Nicholas Gruen] Just run it, turn people over every six months or two years or whatever you decide.

424
00:56:38,266 --> 00:57:08,843
[Nicholas Gruen] I've got a few ideas about how you ultimately create a situation where it could become self-governing in the way that the House of Representatives or the Senate are, these are self-sovereign bodies that make their own decisions and generate their own tradition and go through their own history and their own crises and have their old hands who can help the newcomers and so on.

425
00:57:08,843 --> 00:57:11,438
[Nicholas Gruen] So, anyway, that's what I think.

426
00:57:11,438 --> 00:57:28,146
[Nicholas Gruen] I think we could I think, I mean, there are so many problems of human life where you come up with a really hard problem andor a really ingrained problem, and you basically don't really know how to solve it.

427
00:57:28,146 --> 00:57:40,393
[Nicholas Gruen] You've got a few clues and you try some things out, but this, I don't think, I can't prove this to you, but I don't think that's one of these problems.

428
00:57:40,393 --> 00:57:44,042
[Nicholas Gruen] I think the answer is staring us in the face.

429
00:57:44,042 --> 00:57:47,773
[Nicholas Gruen] And it would be like passing through a portal.

430
00:57:47,773 --> 00:57:51,423
[Nicholas Gruen] Um so that's what we can do, but nobody will.

431
00:57:51,423 --> 00:57:55,235
[Nicholas Gruen] I can't do it because I don't have $10 million.

432
00:57:55,235 --> 00:58:20,134
[Nicholas Gruen] Um and the people who do have all that money run little display shows here and there, and they go and talk to politicians, and the politicians say how good it is of them to do this stuff, and they say, you know, I had this great meeting with senior politician, and and it was really, really good, wasn't it?

433
00:58:20,134 --> 00:58:21,513
[Nicholas Gruen] Okay, yeah, yeah.

434
00:58:21,513 --> 00:58:25,649
[Nicholas Gruen] It was very like the meetings you had 15 years ago.

435
00:58:25,649 --> 00:58:27,920
[Nicholas Gruen] Anyway, I'll leave it there.

436
00:58:29,440 --> 00:58:34,684
[Anthony (Host)] Well, I've got one more question before we hand the mic across to you all.

437
00:58:34,684 --> 00:58:41,486
[Anthony (Host)] So if you're holding some questions, um getting some questions together, um, that's coming next.

438
00:58:41,486 --> 00:58:48,997
[Anthony (Host)] But yeah, I just wanted to finish this part of things with a little bit more of a question to you, Nicole.

439
00:58:48,997 --> 00:58:59,697
[Anthony (Host)] Um, because what Nicholas was saying sort of prompted me to think about, you know, you get this decision-making process where people are chosen by lot.

440
00:58:59,697 --> 00:59:02,389
[Anthony (Host)] I think that's a really exciting idea.

441
00:59:02,389 --> 00:59:10,822
[Anthony (Host)] Um, one of my favorite quotes is from a theologian Kallistos where he says that simply that we need to learn to be free.

442
00:59:10,822 --> 00:59:16,632
[Anthony (Host)] So freedom's just not some automatic thing, there's there's a learning to be free.

443
00:59:16,632 --> 00:59:23,860
[Anthony (Host)] Um, and so the processes that you undertake, we talked a little bit about it um ahead of this evening.

444
00:59:23,860 --> 00:59:29,883
[Anthony (Host)] You talked about, to me, this uh way of education and and learning about the subject.

445
00:59:29,883 --> 00:59:32,292
[Anthony (Host)] So these people are chosen by lot.

446
00:59:32,292 --> 00:59:34,418
[Anthony (Host)] How does that learning happen?

447
00:59:34,418 --> 00:59:39,378
[Anthony (Host)] How does it happen in a way that doesn't sort of bias things too much?

448
00:59:39,378 --> 00:59:43,558
[Anthony (Host)] And we talked a bit about the tyranny of structurelessness.

449
00:59:43,558 --> 00:59:53,124
[Anthony (Host)] So these people are chosen by lot, they're learning about the topic, and then there's a way of facilitating that conversation in a way.

450
00:59:53,124 --> 00:59:59,360
[Anthony (Host)] So I'm interested in a little bit more of the nuts and bolts of how to learn to be free.

451
01:00:01,600 --> 01:00:02,320
[Nicholas Gruen] Easy questions.

452
01:00:04,880 --> 01:00:11,980
[Nicole] Okay, so um first things first, there's uh the experts because we're all experts, the experts for that topic.

453
01:00:11,980 --> 01:00:20,057
[Nicole] There's a lot of I'd say there's a lot of work we do as practitioners to get them ready to be in the room to let go control.

454
01:00:20,057 --> 01:00:25,659
[Nicole] Because that's really important that they're ready to go sort of hand the batten over.

455
01:00:25,659 --> 01:00:31,000
[Nicole] I mean they're there as a resource, but they're not involved in that conversation.

456
01:00:31,000 --> 01:00:33,475
[Nicole] We we put strict guidance around that.

457
01:00:33,475 --> 01:00:35,690
[Nicole] At the moment, you're an observer.

458
01:00:35,690 --> 01:00:39,794
[Nicole] So that means you sit over there and they're doing their stuff.

459
01:00:39,794 --> 01:00:41,617
[Nicole] You're not influencing them.

460
01:00:41,617 --> 01:00:43,832
[Nicole] So that's one part of the process.

461
01:00:43,832 --> 01:00:47,610
[Nicole] For the participants themselves, it's a slow build, right?

462
01:00:47,610 --> 01:00:57,055
[Nicole] You're starting with um getting them working together, understanding that their experience with this, how do you work together to make decisions?

463
01:00:57,055 --> 01:01:00,116
[Nicole] How do you how do you experience that normally?

464
01:01:00,116 --> 01:01:02,201
[Nicole] How might we do it in this room?

465
01:01:02,201 --> 01:01:03,699
[Nicole] Critical thinking bias.

466
01:01:03,699 --> 01:01:05,458
[Nicole] I talked about that before.

467
01:01:05,458 --> 01:01:17,378
[Nicole] Then when you're getting into topic-specific stuff, we do a lot of learning in really small groups, like pairs and threes, and then build, build, build, build, build to a whole group.

468
01:01:17,378 --> 01:01:19,658
[Nicole] We do things called speed dialogue.

469
01:01:19,658 --> 01:01:21,937
[Nicole] It's like speed dating, but better.

470
01:01:21,937 --> 01:01:37,831
[Nicole] And uh it means that they're in small groups with a person who knows a bit more about this from the organization, maybe, maybe from outside the organization, and they sit and have small conversations, and then that person shifts to a new group.

471
01:01:37,831 --> 01:01:43,498
[Nicole] And you just take your time to talk about it and learn about it and unpack information.

472
01:01:43,498 --> 01:01:50,142
[Nicole] You use um, we'll read a little bit and share it with each other and learn from a pack of information.

473
01:01:50,142 --> 01:01:59,261
[Nicole] It's sort of like a slow build from the start, the basics to kind of like becoming really knowledgeable and starting to weigh up trade-offs.

474
01:01:59,261 --> 01:02:13,266
[Nicole] Like we'll go into a conversation that's about, well, you know, you could go this way and it has these risks and benefits, and we could go that way and it has those risks and benefits, and they start to weigh it up.

475
01:02:13,266 --> 01:02:18,086
[Nicole] So you'll literally do whole group conversations like, so how do you feel?

476
01:02:18,086 --> 01:02:20,366
[Nicole] Like, would you stand on this side?

477
01:02:20,366 --> 01:02:26,358
[Nicole] I'd stand over here and half the group over there, and let's have a conversation about that.

478
01:02:26,358 --> 01:02:43,750
[Nicole] So you can see how it's it's built so many little tools in there from individual thinking to pairs to threes to the whole group to kind of dialoguing and trading off, and all of that builds to them, then going, now what do we think is the answer to this collectively?

479
01:02:43,750 --> 01:02:46,225
[Nicole] And we build that up together as well.

480
01:02:46,225 --> 01:03:26,480
[Nicole] And in fact, that moment of choosing what solutions we think are to this problem and building it together for me is the most sacred part of a whole deliberation because that is like the turning point where they go from me to we and they go from that to I understand that, and I understand what you mean by that when you say that, and there are people in the world who are saying, Yeah, we could get AI to do that for us, they could just theme up their ideas, and I'm like, uh-uh, don't touch it, because that choice making the group is doing at that moment has them really understand what they're saying and owning it.

481
01:03:31,120 --> 01:03:33,220
[Anthony (Host)] Well, I don't know about you.

482
01:03:33,220 --> 01:03:35,174
[Anthony (Host)] I got really inspired then.

483
01:03:35,174 --> 01:03:38,287
[Anthony (Host)] So, yeah, that's just that's very exciting.

484
01:03:38,287 --> 01:03:43,065
[Anthony (Host)] Um so thank you, panelists, for answering some of these questions.

485
01:03:43,065 --> 01:03:48,640
[Anthony (Host)] But now it's over to you, um, and I might go over there and hand to Nicholas.

486
01:03:48,960 --> 01:03:58,067
[Audience Q1] So yeah, I wonder before um Nicole, you were mentioning something about um any sort of technological process or any process is going to include or exclude people.

487
01:03:58,067 --> 01:04:04,982
[Audience Q1] And um, Anitra, you mentioned that you know we're burning through like five earths at the moment, the way consumption goes.

488
01:04:04,982 --> 01:04:12,628
[Audience Q1] Uh I'm just thinking though, um any process that we would formulate now, it's only ever going to include you know living people like us.

489
01:04:12,628 --> 01:04:16,282
[Audience Q1] It's not gonna include future generations or animals or anything.

490
01:04:16,282 --> 01:04:20,161
[Audience Q1] Um, and so you see, like people they don't like voting for austerity.

491
01:04:20,161 --> 01:04:30,112
[Audience Q1] So, you know, no matter how sort of democratic a process is, I think Anitra, you were saying we need more democracy in order to solve the problem of burning through five earths.

492
01:04:30,112 --> 01:04:42,480
[Audience Q1] Um, I'm inclined to think that no matter who we select, uh, especially if it's like random juries, aren't we going to end up with people who just keep voting in their own current self-interest against future generations?

493
01:04:46,240 --> 01:04:47,666
[Nicole] It's a great question.

494
01:04:47,666 --> 01:04:57,387
[Nicole] Um, I will say that there are processes we run, depending on the conversation we're having, that is about bringing in the future voice as best we can.

495
01:04:57,387 --> 01:04:59,785
[Nicole] So this has been done a lot in Japan.

496
01:04:59,785 --> 01:05:08,275
[Nicole] Japan, where they'll literally say, We're gonna, there's gonna be a group of you, and we're gonna collectively decide who they are.

497
01:05:08,275 --> 01:05:19,811
[Nicole] Let's say there's five of you, and your job every time we do a process is to act like you're the future, or to act like you're the trees, or to so to to actually bring in voices.

498
01:05:19,811 --> 01:05:29,338
[Nicole] I'm not saying it's perfect, but there are processes that are trying to do that so it's not just coming from my current needs, my current approach.

499
01:05:29,338 --> 01:05:34,134
[Nicole] I totally think it's a real dilemma to think forward and to bring that in.

500
01:05:34,134 --> 01:05:38,800
[Nicole] And I think there are micro processes we can do that might support that.

501
01:05:41,120 --> 01:06:01,028
[Anitra] Yeah, I'd just say that um already to begin with, if you frame questions around the fact that you want to live a one planet lifestyle, well then you've you've already started to think about the future because you're talking about sustainable reproduction.

502
01:06:01,028 --> 01:06:13,520
[Anitra] So I think it's the way that you frame those questions that helps you address future generations having all of the opportunities that they might expect to have.

503
01:06:16,080 --> 01:06:35,001
[Nicholas Gruen] There's something else I think that's worth thinking about, which is that we have been conditioned for for a long time now to think of our vote as we are participants in a market, we are competing with each other in some kind of sense.

504
01:06:35,001 --> 01:06:37,175
[Nicholas Gruen] Now it's quite interesting.

505
01:06:37,175 --> 01:06:46,515
[Nicholas Gruen] I hadn't thought about this, but uh the uh the uh political philosopher Hannah Arendt talks about the secret ballot.

506
01:06:46,515 --> 01:07:09,220
[Nicholas Gruen] Now, the secret, I'm not gonna tell you that the secret ballot's a bad idea, but it is an idea that leans heavily on the idea of privacy and therefore of the individual rather than the res publica, that we get our term republic from the idea the Roman expression the public thing.

507
01:07:09,220 --> 01:07:22,747
[Nicholas Gruen] So republics, and this was big in the minds of the founding fathers in the United States, the to sustain a republic, you need people to be thinking of the public thing.

508
01:07:22,747 --> 01:07:29,994
[Nicholas Gruen] And unfortunately, our more and more our elections are framed around what is in it for me.

509
01:07:29,994 --> 01:07:35,791
[Nicholas Gruen] That's how the media will report on a tax package or anything like that.

510
01:07:35,791 --> 01:07:56,403
[Nicholas Gruen] Uh, and if I know that I'm going into uh I'm going into here I am adopting that sort of language, if I know that I'm going into vote against people who want to uh uh just go with coal-fired power and all the rest of it, I'll be trying to vote against them.

511
01:07:56,403 --> 01:07:58,657
[Nicholas Gruen] It's kind of me versus them.

512
01:07:58,657 --> 01:08:13,070
[Nicholas Gruen] So um but but again, and and I didn't really I did advert to it a bit, but the whole business of being in a citizen jury is that you're not saying who's got 51% of the votes here.

513
01:08:13,070 --> 01:08:16,773
[Nicholas Gruen] You think you're saying what can we live with?

514
01:08:16,773 --> 01:08:19,350
[Nicholas Gruen] And that doesn't mean unanimity.

515
01:08:19,350 --> 01:08:28,690
[Nicholas Gruen] I think Nicole can correct me, but there's a sort of a folk wisdom among many people who they go for 80% majorities.

516
01:08:28,690 --> 01:08:31,669
[Nicholas Gruen] I'd I'd be happy with less than that.

517
01:08:31,669 --> 01:08:46,242
[Nicholas Gruen] But uh, well, you sort of start for 80%, and and no doubt Nicole's had some interesting questions about around that mark, you know, that particular whatever arbitrary mark you pick.

518
01:08:46,242 --> 01:08:53,972
[Nicholas Gruen] But the point is that the psychology of that, the theatrical framing of that, is not a vote out.

519
01:08:53,972 --> 01:09:06,935
[Nicholas Gruen] It's not I'm gonna go and log roll as many votes as I can get, we'll go away for two weeks and I'll be on the phone and I'll be trying to get 51% of these votes.

520
01:09:06,935 --> 01:09:10,880
[Nicholas Gruen] That isn't how human beings relate to each other.

521
01:09:13,600 --> 01:09:17,508
[Godfrey] I think um capitalism gives democracy a bad name.

522
01:09:17,508 --> 01:09:37,048
[Godfrey] Right, when you're talking about how um people might not vote for uh degradation to their quality of life, well, that is about how we try and scrabble around and make a living within a system that just is a is is an undead vampire on the planet.

523
01:09:37,048 --> 01:09:48,373
[Godfrey] And the good life isn't a ready-made meal, the good life isn't getting stuck in traffic, the good life isn't always feeling so freaking tired.

524
01:09:48,373 --> 01:10:06,318
[Godfrey] People pay a lot of money to be able to go on a very exclusive trip to Italy or retire to a place like that when what makes part That a good life is is the local wine or the produce or the time that people can spend together.

525
01:10:06,318 --> 01:10:16,846
[Godfrey] We need to rescue democracy and the good life from a system that is ultimately parasitic upon our present now as well as our future.

526
01:10:16,846 --> 01:10:20,674
[Godfrey] And uh more and more of us are are feeling that.

527
01:10:20,674 --> 01:10:31,680
[Godfrey] Because I I sure as hell is not I'm hoping that my kids' future is not just some other commodity, because that's just deep and depressing.

528
01:10:40,560 --> 01:10:41,274
[Audience Q2] Thank you.

529
01:10:41,274 --> 01:10:46,557
[Audience Q2] I've got a question for Anitra in relation to her expert view on degrowth.

530
01:10:46,557 --> 01:10:51,198
[Audience Q2] How does it play out in the power realm that we're talking about?

531
01:10:51,198 --> 01:10:53,697
[Audience Q2] And how do you apply it personally?

532
01:10:53,697 --> 01:10:56,124
[Audience Q2] Like, do you live in a tiny house?

533
01:10:56,124 --> 01:10:58,266
[Audience Q2] How do you negotiate degrowth?

534
01:10:58,266 --> 01:11:00,622
[Audience Q2] How do you apply it in Melbourne?

535
01:11:00,622 --> 01:11:05,120
[Audience Q2] It's something that as urban planners we're all grappling with.

536
01:11:11,760 --> 01:11:13,588
[Anitra] Great, thank you.

537
01:11:13,588 --> 01:11:17,459
[Anitra] Um okay, so it's a really big topic.

538
01:11:17,459 --> 01:11:33,909
[Anitra] But um degrowth did the degrowth movement basically evolved to address those two big questions of growing inequalities and ecological unsustainabilities.

539
01:11:33,909 --> 01:11:43,048
[Anitra] And degrowth has always had a really strong democratizing principle involved with it.

540
01:11:43,048 --> 01:11:51,112
[Anitra] And there are lots of um different ways in which uh degrowth um is applied.

541
01:11:51,112 --> 01:12:04,659
[Anitra] So the um the handbook that um I co-edited with Vincent Liegey has about 35 chapters, um, but really it's just a tiny segment.

542
01:12:04,659 --> 01:12:19,282
[Anitra] Um, and lots of people are addressing degrowth in a whole variety of different ways depending on which country they live in or whatever.

543
01:12:19,282 --> 01:12:30,571
[Anitra] Um so the general kind of framework, I'll sort of stick to the democracy as a kind of through line in it.

544
01:12:30,571 --> 01:13:19,600
[Anitra] The democracy um part of it um has basically flourished into a general consensus over our futures being commoning, that there's um that we actually let go of private property, that people actually acknowledge that they're just passing through earth and handing it on to other people, but also other animals, other plants, and so that that whole sharing, caring, cooperative way of approaching everything is the way that commoning is instrumentalized, okay?

545
01:13:19,600 --> 01:13:30,782
[Anitra] Using a lot of the kind of ideas of assemblies and having working groups that are enacting these things.

546
01:13:30,782 --> 01:13:43,899
[Anitra] At the basis of it, it's essential that we we're actually living as modestly as we can, but also as convivially as we can.

547
01:13:43,899 --> 01:14:04,543
[Anitra] In other words, just loving one another's company, um, actually getting a lot of creative nourishment ourselves out of thinking around problems of using less materials and energy to do things.

548
01:14:04,543 --> 01:14:12,499
[Anitra] Um I could keep on going, but I'm just aware that we've got you know more.

549
01:14:12,499 --> 01:14:13,037
[Anitra] Yeah.

550
01:14:13,037 --> 01:14:31,530
[Anitra] Um in terms of Australia and trying to and trying to do degrowth, there um is D-Growth Network Australia, which meets um online um different parts of Australia every month.

551
01:14:31,530 --> 01:14:50,239
[Anitra] There's a NAM DG Growth, um, and NAM DGrowth does really great things like have festivals at Ceres with a lot of like groups like Well Being Alliance and the kinds of policy.

552
01:14:50,239 --> 01:15:11,527
[Anitra] I would say, or programs, or whatever that degrowth follow, are things like universal basic services being applied so that everyone has immediate access to basic services, but there's a limit on it.

553
01:15:11,527 --> 01:15:15,936
[Anitra] You were talking about tiny houses, yeah.

554
01:15:15,936 --> 01:15:37,009
[Anitra] So really the uh in Australia in planning and stuff, we've um often had kind of like minimums for sizes of housing and number of bedrooms before we call it overcrowding and all that kind of thing.

555
01:15:37,009 --> 01:15:49,266
[Anitra] Well, DGrowth turns that on its head and says that we need to have the maximum amount of space that people occupy.

556
01:15:49,266 --> 01:16:29,371
[Anitra] And uh also uh I mean we've done a there is a sort of housing for degrowth book which sort of goes through a lot of that that the planning, but another kind of edge on all of that is decentralization, so that we actually don't live in cities that are sort of cocoons away from nature and seeing how things are grown and how animals are killed and all of that kind of thing.

557
01:16:29,371 --> 01:16:37,865
[Anitra] But rather we're really close and all of us involved with growing our own food.

558
01:16:37,865 --> 01:16:57,003
[Anitra] Um also kind of speaking earlier with someone who's been in Nepal where people are readily all together make their own houses, repair their own houses, all of that kind of thing.

559
01:16:57,003 --> 01:17:48,720
[Anitra] So there's a lot more of doing it together and planning that's kind of most of the planning regulations and building regulations are all created for industry and people are actually not allowed to do stuff, and what we need is again to turn that on its head and have enabling policies so that people can always with the um modification that you want, safety and security, but um people being involved in building their own houses, in growing their own food, and that kind of thing.

560
01:17:49,280 --> 01:17:52,402
[Anthony (Host)] That's fascinating.

561
01:17:52,402 --> 01:17:53,388
[Anthony (Host)] Sorry.

562
01:17:53,388 --> 01:17:55,360
[Anthony (Host)] Pardon my um

563
01:17:58,000 --> 01:18:06,022
[Audience Q3] thank you very much to all four of you for for sharing what democracy could be and and your experiences with that.

564
01:18:06,022 --> 01:18:17,351
[Audience Q3] What I miss now is that we begin to talk about the urgency of how we change things because you talked about uh democracy gave us Hitler, democracy gave us Trump.

565
01:18:17,351 --> 01:18:27,343
[Audience Q3] In Germany two weeks ago, democracy gave us alternative for Deutschland, which is a party with close affiliations with neo-Nazis, with Russia.

566
01:18:27,343 --> 01:18:29,665
[Audience Q3] And and the world keeps changing.

567
01:18:29,665 --> 01:18:32,058
[Audience Q3] Democracy has given us one nation.

568
01:18:32,058 --> 01:18:45,920
[Audience Q3] So, how do we move from sitting in a fringe corner talking about what democracy could look like theoretically into something about the something urgent and something about the how, how do we do it.

569
01:18:49,920 --> 01:19:00,817
[Nicholas Gruen] Well, um my suggestion is that uh we persuade uh we make some donations ourselves, but we try to persuade uh philanthropists with the money.

570
01:19:00,817 --> 01:19:11,791
[Nicholas Gruen] It's not that expensive, maybe two million to run a citizen assembly for six months to a year, uh, and then to fundraise on the back of that.

571
01:19:11,791 --> 01:19:23,778
[Nicholas Gruen] And basically to run and and my point about the point my point in suggesting this is that people need to see this hap they need to see this thing working.

572
01:19:23,778 --> 01:19:38,333
[Nicholas Gruen] Um and they need to see that it they need to feel their own agency and the way in which um it can make better decisions in certain circumstances than the people they see in front of them.

573
01:19:38,333 --> 01:19:46,739
[Nicholas Gruen] I don't know, I mean, probably most Australians don't know that much about AUKUS, but it's a kind of a joke.

574
01:19:46,739 --> 01:19:52,810
[Nicholas Gruen] It's just a sort of mad exercise where Scott Morrison signed up to this thing.

575
01:19:52,810 --> 01:19:55,690
[Nicholas Gruen] I wasn't that farcical at that stage.

576
01:19:55,690 --> 01:20:04,251
[Nicholas Gruen] The Labour Party, as far as I can see, signed up to it because they didn't want the election to be about that.

577
01:20:04,251 --> 01:20:23,943
[Nicholas Gruen] And then, a bit like Tony Abbott, who won election by getting rid of carbon pricing, and somehow couldn't understand that he then needed to somehow replicate something a bit like a carbon price because otherwise things would look more and more farcical.

578
01:20:23,943 --> 01:20:28,380
[Nicholas Gruen] So anyway, the Labour Party didn't take this opportunity.

579
01:20:28,380 --> 01:20:38,732
[Nicholas Gruen] They didn't get into power and say, I'm shocked, I'm shocked that more work hasn't been done on this and we can't go ahead with this.

580
01:20:38,732 --> 01:20:41,690
[Nicholas Gruen] There are just oodles of these things.

581
01:20:41,690 --> 01:20:47,605
[Nicholas Gruen] So that's one of the things that a standing citizen assembly could be doing.

582
01:20:47,605 --> 01:20:57,179
[Nicholas Gruen] So I'm my idea is to try to organize collisions between the way we're doing politics now and another way of doing politics.

583
01:20:57,179 --> 01:21:04,962
[Nicholas Gruen] Now that isn't, I mean, the these people have no formal power, but we still live in an open society.

584
01:21:04,962 --> 01:21:07,530
[Nicholas Gruen] They still can have a big impact.

585
01:21:07,530 --> 01:21:22,942
[Nicholas Gruen] I think if we had had a body like this in 2013, Tony Abbott would have found it very difficult to get the abolition of carbon pricing through the Senate because it wasn't actually that easy for him.

586
01:21:22,942 --> 01:21:24,810
[Nicholas Gruen] It was only a few votes.

587
01:21:24,810 --> 01:21:35,084
[Nicholas Gruen] And if you have 60 odd percent, 65% of Australians, random Australians, ordinary Australians, figuring out that this makes no sense.

588
01:21:35,084 --> 01:21:44,424
[Nicholas Gruen] Just by the way, we are paying $12 billion more tax every year than we would have if we hadn't abolished carbon pricing.

589
01:21:44,424 --> 01:21:49,483
[Nicholas Gruen] The revenue from the carbon pricing was going to rise inexorably.

590
01:21:49,483 --> 01:21:54,231
[Nicholas Gruen] I hadn't done the calculations lately, it may be $15 billion.

591
01:21:54,231 --> 01:21:56,799
[Nicholas Gruen] They would have figured that out.

592
01:21:56,799 --> 01:22:08,163
[Nicholas Gruen] They would have said to the people who wanted to get rid of a carbon price, right, so we're going to be on the hook to pay this much more revenue.

593
01:22:08,163 --> 01:22:20,461
[Nicholas Gruen] In other words, Tony Abbott would have had more, a bigger challenge on his hands than getting through a 10-minute radio interview, which was all he had to do.

594
01:22:20,461 --> 01:22:21,862
[Nicholas Gruen] That's the system.

595
01:22:21,862 --> 01:22:23,808
[Nicholas Gruen] So that's what I suggest.

596
01:22:23,808 --> 01:22:48,092
[Nicholas Gruen] And I'm I think that having another citizen assembly in an era, I mean, the some of the things that Nicole's talking about is rather different because Yarra Valley Water citizen assemblies are sponsored by Yarra Valley Water, and they're trying to build a system that has some social license and social buy-in.

597
01:22:48,092 --> 01:22:50,193
[Nicholas Gruen] That's different to say Mr.

598
01:22:50,193 --> 01:22:56,732
[Nicholas Gruen] Macron's, oh God, what or for that matter, Julia Gillard's, oh my God, what do I do?

599
01:22:56,732 --> 01:23:07,784
[Nicholas Gruen] I know I'll have a citizen assembly and that'll diffuse the situation, and then a year goes by, and then that everyone's got the same dilemma.

600
01:23:07,784 --> 01:23:09,418
[Nicholas Gruen] I'm more sympathetic.

601
01:23:09,418 --> 01:23:15,489
[Nicholas Gruen] I'm not telling you this story on the basis that Emmanuel Macron is a bad guy.

602
01:23:15,489 --> 01:23:18,136
[Nicholas Gruen] He's facing a bunch of incentives.

603
01:23:18,136 --> 01:23:31,912
[Nicholas Gruen] And he thinks a citizen assembly will be a good idea, and then he looks at lots of the recommendations and he thinks I can't go to an election and win if I've done those things.

604
01:23:31,912 --> 01:23:41,720
[Nicholas Gruen] So that's so with kind of charity for all, if I can quote Abraham Lincoln, we can build ways in which people see these things.

605
01:23:41,720 --> 01:23:53,939
[Nicholas Gruen] And what I'm hoping is that if people become familiar with this, then minority parties like the Teals and so on might win their choosing between governments.

606
01:23:53,939 --> 01:24:05,381
[Nicholas Gruen] So I ran round the corridors of Parliament coming up to the last election thinking that the Teals might have the balance of power, how wrong I was.

607
01:24:05,381 --> 01:24:21,337
[Nicholas Gruen] But had they had the balance of power, I was saying to them, you should say that one of your demands, if you you can say to Albo and say to Dutton, first thing on my auction is a standing citizen assembly.

608
01:24:21,337 --> 01:24:27,564
[Nicholas Gruen] You don't have to promise to do anything, just have a standing citizen assembly.

609
01:24:27,564 --> 01:24:30,599
[Nicholas Gruen] Well, they weren't close to doing that.

610
01:24:30,599 --> 01:24:36,203
[Nicholas Gruen] Um, so there's a lot of socialization to do, a lot of familiarity to do.

611
01:24:36,203 --> 01:24:37,760
[Nicholas Gruen] I'll leave it there.

612
01:24:40,960 --> 01:24:51,939
[Godfrey] Um I was just gonna say, I think the a lot of my work with the Solidarity Wedge is about how do we have a radical labour movement strategy that meets the moment.

613
01:24:51,939 --> 01:24:54,326
[Godfrey] Uh and it's actually not that hard.

614
01:24:54,326 --> 01:25:07,896
[Godfrey] Anyone can do it if you if you think through the the parameters, but What I've rubbed up against is that uh even from a position of relative privilege within the Labour movement, that that just died.

615
01:25:07,896 --> 01:25:09,942
[Godfrey] Like it it didn't go anywhere.

616
01:25:09,942 --> 01:25:31,422
[Godfrey] And I think the reason why it didn't go anywhere is because in order to be able to do that, you need to radically change the balance of internal power between uh exec like labor movement leaders like I was, uh, no longer, and rank and file delegates and organizers and the operational autonomy that they would have.

617
01:25:31,422 --> 01:25:34,764
[Godfrey] And that's an incredibly threatening proposition.

618
01:25:34,764 --> 01:25:50,175
[Godfrey] And so I've rapidly came to the idea that, well, there is a cybernetic question here about organizational design and what sort of organizations do we build that are fit for purpose to meet the moment that we're that we are in.

619
01:25:50,175 --> 01:26:30,000
[Godfrey] And I know it's way more complex than this, but I look at the the uh rise of the the radical right uh and their their increase in popularity as symptomatic of the moment that we're in, that it's almost as if people in this quite atomized, alienated times are feeling, whether it's not a sense of belonging, whether it's a material uh issue or insecurity or whatever else it is, uh, a sense of frustration with the system, and there is a flashing red button that they can hit, and that flashing red button here is one nation, and then AFT elsewhere, and Trump, and so on, and so forth.

620
01:26:30,000 --> 01:26:35,455
[Godfrey] And the solution has to be what is it that is not just that flashing red button?

621
01:26:35,455 --> 01:26:55,845
[Godfrey] Because it's a very uh coarse way of doing it, but it's also a brilliant systemic intervention on the system as it is, because you push the flashing red button to express your frustration, and then really what you're doing is just reinforcing the existing, the existing structures within the system.

622
01:26:55,845 --> 01:27:01,846
[Godfrey] So it's this like brilliantly designed negative feedback loop to reinforce what we have.

623
01:27:01,846 --> 01:27:06,960
[Godfrey] And we've got a people need something else that they can do, anything else.

624
01:27:11,760 --> 01:27:22,197
[Anitra] Um I think that it's a trap to think that um the political realm is where the where the decisions are being made.

625
01:27:22,197 --> 01:27:31,618
[Anitra] The biggest decisions that are being made in the system that we live in are being made by capitalists.

626
01:27:31,618 --> 01:27:43,902
[Anitra] And this is one, this is at the base of the problem of us not being able to confront climate change and all of these kinds of things.

627
01:27:43,902 --> 01:27:47,134
[Anitra] Politicians have very little power.

628
01:27:47,134 --> 01:27:55,909
[Anitra] It's the people who have the money that invest in um big projects who are making the decisions.

629
01:27:55,909 --> 01:28:02,005
[Anitra] Recently there's been a lot of media coverage of the data centers.

630
01:28:02,005 --> 01:28:18,168
[Anitra] And um the conversation uh had an article, I think it was either this this morning or yesterday, on trying to answer the nine big questions that people had about data centers.

631
01:28:18,168 --> 01:28:24,356
[Anitra] Number one question: how come we never knew it was going to happen?

632
01:28:24,356 --> 01:28:38,303
[Anitra] Well, we don't know things are going to happen because we're never involved, and the politicians aren't involved with what our futures are going to be.

633
01:28:38,303 --> 01:28:40,519
[Anitra] Capitalists decide that.

634
01:28:40,519 --> 01:28:50,956
[Anitra] They're the ones who are out there deciding, well, we'll use this technology, we'll do this, that, and the other.

635
01:28:50,956 --> 01:28:56,590
[Anitra] And it's the fifth anniversary of AUKUS, actually, um, today.

636
01:28:56,590 --> 01:28:57,052
[Anitra] Okay.

637
01:28:57,052 --> 01:29:01,301
[Anitra] Um really good example of all of that as well.

638
01:29:01,301 --> 01:29:35,659
[Anitra] A lot of what AUKUS is going to do is to put money in certain people's pockets, and it's also going to make us feel more terrified and feel more disciplined and feel less powerful, and as if we can do and say something, we're the ones who actually have to decide that yes, it is urgent, and we're going to have to break the rules of this society in order to change things.

639
01:29:35,659 --> 01:29:40,000
[Anitra] You can't have change if you don't have change.

640
01:29:40,960 --> 01:29:43,474
[Anthony (Host)] I reckon we've got two more questions.

641
01:29:43,474 --> 01:29:47,840
[Anthony (Host)] I did have uh sorry to take over, but I'll just throw over to you.

642
01:29:49,040 --> 01:29:49,747
[Audience Q4] Good evening.

643
01:29:49,747 --> 01:29:50,889
[Audience Q4] Thank you, panelists.

644
01:29:50,889 --> 01:29:56,545
[Audience Q4] It's so nice to hear people reflect on this idea that actually we're can we we're innately good at this.

